Subject: RE: NT Telnet daemon

Brian,
 Currently, NT really only supports Remote Node via RAS.  You can =
connect to a Unix system, with TCP-IP as the protocol, and then use =
Telnet to login. If you're trying to get to an ISP, you'll need some GUI =
to help out over MS Winsock TCP stack.  I believe that Mosaic runs on =
Alpha, but you weren't specific about the platform.

If you're looking to "login" remotely to NT, there are a few products =
available for the Intel side of things.  Symantec's PC Anywhere will =
allow you to execute a remote session via modem.  It's pretty slow,  =
single user, and there's no good way to get things back to the system =
you're sitting in front of.  Citrix WinFrame allows multi-user access to =
NT.  It's FAST, you can have multiple, independent NT sessions executing =
all at the same time.  AND if you're remotely executing say Word on the =
system you're controlling remotely, you can map back to your local =
drives AND printers to save or get output where you are sitting!! =
[www.citrix.com]  Unfortunately, although Microsoft has agreed to =
distribute the client side of this with every MS operating system, this =
is an Intel only product so far.

cjm

