From rog@therion.lamc.utexas.edu Wed May  1 12:30:04 1996 CDT
Message-ID: <1996q2-0052.alphant@archive.invalid>
X-Archive-Original-Message-ID: <3187A431@smtpgate.eagan.ncs.com>
Date: Wed, 1 May 1996 12:24:05 -0500 (CDT)
From: Robert Parker <rog@therion.lamc.utexas.edu>
Subject: Re: NT 3.51 Mac File Server Performance Mystery
In-Reply-To: <v01530504adab05fc4508@[138.77.15.81]>

Well, I finally stumbled upon the registry key values to increase the 
Paged and NonPaged memory limits (ie cache) for the MacFile Server.  This 
seemed to increase performance to some slight degree, but did not 
alleviate the problem.  I also ran CHKDSK /F on all the relevent volumes, 
and it turned up nothing.  In addition, I upgraded all the client macs to 
the latest version of AppleShare Workstation (3.6.2), which resulted in a 
slight performance increase, but once again, did very little to fix the 
problem.

I was about to take a chainsaw to our whole network when i got response 
from someone on usenet who mentioned he had a very similar problem with 
his NT box and that he managed to alleviate the MacFile Server 
performance problem by merely disabling the screen saver on the console.  
My first reaction to this was of course immediate skepticism.  This guy 
must be looney.  But seeing as how I had few options left, I went ahead 
and disabled the screen saver..AND THE DAMN THING WORKED.

Performance is now back up to the old standards, and even surpasses it to 
some degree.  I tested all the screen savers to see if the MacFile Server 
performance was affected more or less by different ones and I found that 
they all gave a significant hit to the performance of the MacFile Server 
(and no other service, as far as I can tell), but in particular the OpenGL 
screensavers brought about the greatest performance hit.

This sounded like pure coincidence to me at first, but after doing quite 
a bit of tesing, it seemed to be rather consistent.  Why on earth would 
the screensaver affect the performance of a file service, and why the 
MacFile Service in particular?  Are there any other documented cases of 
this?  

Once again Im using an AlphaServer 1000 4/266 w/ NT3.51, Service Pack 4 
and using the onboard cirrus logic video system.

Any ideas at all on this?

