From jwl@ntmail.zso.dec.com Wed May  1 14:30:56 1996 CDT
Message-ID: <1996q2-0060.alphant@archive.invalid>
X-Archive-Original-Message-ID: <c=US%a=_%p=Digital%l=NTMAIL2-960501190519Z-2606@ntmail3.zso.dec.com>
Date: Wed, 1 May 1996 12:05:19 -0700
From: James Livingston <jwl@ntmail.zso.dec.com>
Subject: RE: NT 3.51 Mac File Server Performance Mystery

Robert,

Bingo!  You cannot imagine how hard I fought to get that !#!*&&*@!
Cirrus Logic VGA part replaced by something even a little more
intelligent, but the product manager would have none of it; a smarter
chip costs more, and the 1000 is a low-end, low-cost server, he said; 
graphics performance is not an issue in a server, he said.  Yeah, right!
 

Well, it is a little known fact that screen-savers get extremely high
execution priority, even in a server environment; with that chip as the
display device, everything graphic is done with CPU cycles.  In the case
of most screen savers, they're emulated x86 CPU cycles, as well.  In the
case of OpenGL screen savers, you can soak up lots and lots of CPU
cycles, as you have discovered.

Thanks for being persistent, and adding to our useful folklore; I knew
about many of the problems caused by the Cirrus Logic on-board VGA,
e.g., scrolling a console prompt screen, but this one is subtle, and
bears repeated mention.  The best solution is not to run a screen saver
on your server.

Cheers,
James

