>     1) what is the best way to restart the system when this occurs?
>      I have been using the restart button but then no error logs seem to be 
>produced.

When NT "blue-screens", it's Big Red Switch time.  You have to do a hardware
reset.  Ctrl+Alt+Del won't work.

To capture a log of the failure for Microsoft to examine, go to Control
Panel, System. Click on the Recovery button.  From the Recovery dialog you
can choose to have an event written to the system log or choose to have the
system perform a core dump for someone at Microsoft to examine.  The next
time the system crashes, you'll have a snapshot of the moment of failure.
You can't do anything with the core dump file, it's only useful to MS.

>     2) Although I don't have an exact record it seems to occur after we run 
>our weekly backup to tape.  I had updated the scsi tape driver but I wonder 
>if there is a newer one?
>
>     3) What is the best way to upgrade our current nt 3.5 to the latest nt 
>3.51?

Subscribe to the Microsoft Developer Network, or the Microsoft Technical
Library.  One or both of those includes the latest versions of Windows 95
and Windows NT workstation for all platforms, mailed to you quarterly.  The
latest retail release of Windows NT is now version 3.51, service pack 4.
Windows NT 4.0 is also in beta.

-Danny

