owner-alphant wrote:
> What CPU is faster? DEC Alpha or Pentium Pro 200mhz(single and dual
>configurations.
>

A single 300MHz Alpha is faster than a single 200MHz PPro for integer   
performance.  A single processor Alpha is faster than 3 PPro processors   
for floating point performance; with the 400 MHz chip, that should   
approch 4x the performance.  The 500 MHz chip that will be available by   
year's end should make the integer performance close to double the 200   
MHz chip's.

A dual processor Alpha is faster than a dual processor PPro.  A quad   
processor Alpha is faster than a quad processor PPro.  Now, if we only   
had dual and quad workstations instead of just servers...  Digital has a   
history of introducing dual processor workstations only when they have a   
perceived performance disadvantage -- they like to keep the higher-end   
stuff in the more expensive server boxes (better profit margins, recoup   
more of the development costs, helps the stock value improve, keeps the   
company solvent, etc.).  The workstation group in Digital would *love* to   
build a dual processor system (I wish I could see what's in the lab right   
now...), and with the relase of the R10000, the PA8000, and the PPro, we   
may be getting into a situation where that may become a reality.

We can only hope and say "Yes please!" and buy them when they become   
available (ah, that's the real key -- money talks!)

Of course, if money is no object, I'd love to have a quad processor 4100   
at 400 MHz ea. with 1 GByte or 2 of RAM and 20 GBytes of FW SCSI disks   
and a pair of PowerStorm 3D graphics adapters, but $100K+ for a   
workstation doesn't fit into my budget right now...  Plus, it would look   
kinda clunky in my office.

Cheers,
Aaron
