On Fri, 10 May 1996, Dave Harrold, CAM Operations, Ext. 25639 wrote:

> Hi All,
> 
> We are in the processes of testing an AlphaNT machine to use as an AppleShare 
> server for the Mac Support Group here.  I would like to ask about your 
> configurations if you are going something similar.  This is the first NT box we 
> have, so none of us are experts on the operation of an NT box.
> 
> Here is how I have configured the machine:
> 
> Alphaserver 1000 4/266
> Window NT 3.51 Service Pack 4
...
> 
> The mirror sets are setup using the software based raid in Disk Administrator.
> 
> I have seen the reports of excellent performance of the AppleShare service, but
> we are seeing pretty poor performance.
> 
> Is there anything to change in the configuration of the machine?  Any
> parameters that can be set/changed on the AppleShare Server?  Any Suggestions
> at all?

We have almost exactly the same setup as you do, apart from the software 
RAID setup and having an RZ29B and an RZ28M.

We have approximately 90 client Macs sharing 5 separate AppleShare 
Volumes.  AppleShare performance is incredible.  The AlpaServer 
outperforms the Apple Workgroup Server 8150/132/32 running AppleShare 4.2 we 
tested hands down.  Almost 20% faster in file access, mounting, copying, 
and file execution.  Its been in service since January with very few 
complaints.

Not only does our AlphaServer serve as an AppleShare file server and 
AppleTalk seed router (bridging two subnets-two zones per subnet) but it also 
is our main weberver (Netscape Comm Server 1.12), FTP server, domain 
controller, netBIOS/netBEUI/SMB/IPX file server, RAS Server, and just 
recently, an MS SQL Server 6.5 server (eval version).  Since most of our 
clients are Macs, the MacFile server does seem to get the most demand, but 
even with all of these services running the MacFile server hardly skips a 
beat (though you must admit 64Mb is quite a bit to work with).

As far as tips go, Firstly: DO NOT UNDER ANY CIRCUMSTANCES RUN A SCREEN 
SAVER ON THE CONSOLE.  I found out the hard way that screen savers suck up 
obscene amounts of processor time, and apparently the MacFile server is one 
of the services most most heavily hit by it.  Dont even run a screen blank 
saver.  Just leave it on the login screen or dim the monitor when its 
unattended. 
 
Secondly: You can  edit/add the registry service parameter entries for 
increased cache/memory allocation to the MacFile server.  These are 
"PagedMemLimit" and "NonPagedMemLimit" and their description can be found 
on page 128 of the Services for Macintosh manual.

Lastly: Alpha/NT makes an excellent AppleShare server for RevRdist, if 
you happen to have labs that need identical/auomatically maintained 
machines.  We setup a mirror volume for 30 7500/100's in a lab and set 
them all to siultaneously rebuild from the volume with RevRdist.  The 
~350Mb per machine rebuild job only took ~15 minutes...all concurrently 
copying from the same AppleShare volume.

