Date: Tue, 14 May 1996 10:27:23 -0700
From: James Livingston <jwl@ntmail.zso.dec.com>
Subject: RE: Dual booting Digital UNIX and NT

Kevin,

Yes, what you want is possible, but there are some things to watch out
for, and areas where errors can be especially costly.  In particular,
when you run Windows NT Disk Manager, you are asked if you want to write
a header on this disk it found that doesn't have one; it will assure you
that this will do no harm to any operating system on the disk.  That's
true, if the operating system happens to be DOS or OS/2; it's NOT true
for UNIX, so it's especially critical that everyone who might run Disk
Manager is aware always to answer that question "NO".

In general terms, you must get your AlphaServer 2100 firmware updated to
include the 4.43 revision, or later, of the NT ARC firmware.  After
that, prepare for installation of Windows NT by removing your UNIX
disks; you can reinstall them after you have installed Windows NT (but
see below).  Finally, you can select "Install NT from CD-ROM" from the
NT ARC "Supplementary menu...", which you select on the NT ARC boot
screen.

Just remember, Windows NT does not understand UNIX disk structure, and
vice-versa, so it would be best to pull your UNIX disks when you run
Windows NT, and your Windows NT disks when you run UNIX.   If you're
using the built-in disk subsystem, that's pretty straightforward, with
proper labeling, and it deals with the Disk Manager problem in the first
paragraph. 

Cheers,
James

