You can also set your current C: drive to have no letter assigned to it   
from the Disk Manager.  (IMHO, this should be an option during setup if   
you select an NTFS partition, if not the default action.)

Don't forget that moving your D: drive to C: will break all of your   
applications and many system components.  You can look through the   
registry, especially the HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet for   
all references to D: and change them to C:, then check all of your   
layered apps, but that will be extremely tedious and prone to failure.

Aaron
