I may have great benchmark news for Alpha fans.  I just received the =
latest Ansys Benchmarks.  To give you some background, Ansys is an =
Engineering Analysis tool (Finite Element Analysis).  Generally FEA is =
highly CPU (floating point) intense.  Ansys Inc runs a standard set of =
batch files as benchmarks across most of the hardware platforms that =
Ansys is ported to.

				OS	LS1 (CPU)	LS2 (CPU)	LS3 (CPU) 	LS4 (CPU)	LS5 (CPU)

DEC AlphaStation 600 5/333	UNIX	16		89		281		648		622
192MB mem

Intergraph TDZ-400 		NT	60		386		1232		2836		2642
(dual P Pro 200)
128 MB mem

Usually they run the Alpha boxes in both UNIX and NT.  The results are =
usually within one or two seconds of each other.  To be honest, it is =
difficult to multithread this type of analysis. As a result, the dual =
processor setup didn't help the P Pro system very much.

I am running an Alpha Clone (DCG Computers) 21164/300Mhz with 256 megs =
ram.  I've had run times cut from 13 hours on a Pentium 133 with 128 =
megs of ram to 20 minutes with my current system.  I have no doubt that =
the P Pro 200 has it's applications were it outperforms the Alpha boxes. =
 Ansys isn't one of them.  I am also starting to use Unigraphics.  I'll =
let you all know how performance is when I have a chance to compare the =
Alpha to other boxes.  The UG guy who helped install it went home last =
night to benchmark his HP Unix box against my Alpha.  He was more than a =
bit surprised <grin>.

As a side bar, I haven't dealt with Polywell before.  I have 2 Alpha =
systems now from DCG.  I've been running with one for 8 months now, and =
the system has performed solid as a rock.  I have run benchmark tests =
that show it runs slightly faster than equivalent DEC systems and more =
than marginally better in Ansys benchmarks against "equivalent" Raptor =
systems.  I would say there is a big difference between clone =
manufacturers. =20

