In the configuration table of the article on page 85 the Polywell
P6 system is described as a dual with two P6-200s (the 512k cache
versions).  That would also explain why it did so well vs. the
other systems which included ALRs Quad P6-166 (also 512k cache)
and Compaq's dual P5-166 (2M cache per proc).  Fortunately, or
unfortunately, server applications and benchmarks scale well on MP
if the OS, application, and drivers are multithreaded.  Comparing
a single processor system to a set of MPs should yield results like
those posted.

The network interface listed for the Alpha was a Cogent quad ethernet
card.  This card uses 4 Digital PCI ethernet controllers behind
a Digital PCI-PCI bridge chip.  I do not know if this would have
any disadvantage vs. the multi-adapter designs used in the other
systems but I do not think it would be significant.

I did not see the reference to the 64MB recognition problem in the
article, but if it is there it would explain another significant
bottleneck on the Alpha system that could severely impact performance.
If in fact the system reported only seeing 64MB when 128MB were
installed, it is likely due to a mis-jumpered memory selector.  The
board that the Polywell Alpha system is based on uses 2 'banks' of
memory, each 128 bits wide.  If only one bank is populated, it is seen
as one buswidth to the processor, and cache refills take four
transactions from memory.  If the second bank is populated, the
jumper tells the processor that it is there, AND TO TREAT IT AS
A 256 BIT WIDE MEMORY SYSTEM.  This significantly reduces the
cache refill time from memory and dramatically improves latency
to memory bound benchmarks that access random locations in
memory (which is characteristic of the two benchmarks in the
InfoWorld article).  It would be interesting to see if this was
