Some commentary on the Byte article:

What Byte found is that while their CPU benchmark suite (BYTEmark 2.0)
shows the 333MHz 21164 running almost twice as fast as the 200MHz
P6 in both integer and fp tests, the applications performance was
less than desirable (or expected).

The applications test suite that was used is the BAPCo SYSmark NT V1.0,
which is a good start at cross-platform benchmarking but has some
severe artifacts in its current version (that BAPCo is working on
fixing in 2.0).

SYSmark NT was developed about a year or so ago and one of its main
constraints was that it support native applications on all of the
NT capable platforms, including X86, Alpha, MIPs and PowerPC.  At the
time (and still today) the MIPs and PowerPC platforms were a severe
limiter to the selections available.  The result was a list of
only 5 applications (all other BAPCo benchmarks typically have 10-20
in different application groups representing real user typical
workloads.  this could not be done in this case).  The 5 ran on
Alpha, MIPs and X86 at the time with promises of possible ports
to PowerPC in the near future.  The one exception to this is
the presentation application, PowerPoint from Microsoft, which
is still not native on any RISC and runs under emulation in the
benchmark (pretty well considering this constraint) on all but Intel.

The other applications are MAXEDA as the CAD application, Word and Excel
from Microsoft and Texim Project.

The supposition is that the CAD application takes advantage of 
intensive FP activity and should shine on Alpha.  In fact it is
a PC board routing package that does its math in integer format.  
The Intel version was re-released just before the release of the
benchmark to get those numbers as high as possible.

Texim project is from a small company in Texas, and I believe the
application has been discontinued.  The Intel version runs
dramatically well for various compiler reasons (we have played with
rebuilt versions here that provide 40%-100% improvement).  However
since the product is not being rebuilt with current compiler
technology the results are what was shipping when it was a(n unused)
product.

Word and Excel are scripted to spend most of their time writing
results to the screen too fast for any human to care.  This video
bottleneck accounts for some of the lack of performance advantage
of Alpha in these kinds of applications and the rest is probably
attributable to the differences between hand crafted Intel code
tuned for 10 years vs. a pretty straight recompile on Alpha.  Newer
code doesn't behave this way (usually) since the modern Intel
processors do pretty well with compiled code without a lot of
hacking to fix architectural anomalies.

The next version of SYSmark NT should be dramatically better (particularly
if users of this list participate by providing input to the request for
input to Paul Hakala of a few weeks ago).  It will include applications
with broader appeal such as real CAD, and animation rendering
programs.  In addition, it looks like the MIPs requirement will be
withdrawn since it is very limiting and there is no MIPs participation
in BAPCo as of late.  PowerPC will still probably lock out the
use of some popular applications like AutoCAD and Lotus Notes but
the options are much broader than they were before.

Hope this at least adds some understanding why some of the
benchmark data out there seems contradictory to the performance
many Alpha system users see every day.  I am naturally biased
on this topic but it is a situation that I see getting better
every day and the future looks quite bright as NT4.0, FX32
and some inexpensive Alphas start shipping in the next year.

Aaron Bauch
Manager, Technical Marketing
Digital Semiconductor
a business unit of Digital Equipt. Corp.

