From digital1@enet.net Fri May 24 10:55:56 1996 CDT
Message-ID: <1996q2-0337.alphant@archive.invalid>
X-Archive-Original-Message-ID: <01BB4954.8975CD60@troll.au.af.mil>
Date: Fri, 24 May 1996 08:52:43 -0700
From: Charles J. McCallion <digital1@enet.net>
Subject: NT Domain size and alphas, again

Servers [vice Domain Controllers] can be used as file, print or =
application servers [read powerful / fast systems].  They don't =
participate in user account replication or user logon validation nor do =
they have the overhead of being a domain controller.  You can still use =
them for RAS, Mac Services and have software fault tolerance.  You CAN'T =
upgrade a server to a Domain Controller without a complete reinstall.  =
Think of a server as a workstation on steroids - you even get the =
PowerUser account!

Domain Controllers:
Primary keeps the "master" list of accounts and all of the security =
policies for the domain.

Backups will replicate the accounts database [if you set them up =
properly] and have the same SID as the PDC.  These will validate user =
domain logons before a PDC depending on activity levels.

If you use BDCs on your network you really should be using "per seat" =
licensing, i.e. each client has a license instead of the "concurrent =
connection" scheme.  Thus, only a server license would be necessary.

A i486 / 50 supposedly could handle up to 15,000 users if it's only =
function were as a Domain Controller!

Later,
cjm

