From:  Lambert Hofstra[SMTP:lamhof@thijssen.nl]
Sent:  Wednesday, May 22, 1996 3:49 AM
To:  Charles J. McCallion
Cc:  alphant@garply.com
Subject:  Re: FW: NT Domain size and alphas

Hello all,

Adding a small i486 as a PDC is a good solution as long as you take a =
"real"
server, not a desktop PC that is used as a server.
I often speak with people that do not know the difference between a =
system
that is designed as a server (power supply, ECC memory, expandability), =
and
a system that is designed as a desktop PC (small, cheap, limited power
supply, no ECC, only parity memory if you're lucky).

I would advice you to use the 2100 as a PDC (and file- and printserver).

You might use a simple intel box (or multiple intels) as the BDC (Backup
Domain Controller). Clients will automatically use a BDC if one exists =
(and
not the PDC) for normal logon. If one of these BDC's crashes, the =
clients
will continue to work. The BDC's are only caches, and won't store =
important
data. All the data will be stored on the real server (the PDC).
You will need a NT server license for the BDC's

If you need fast throughput, you should concentrate on your disk =
subsystem:
adding multiple fast ethernet controllers won't help if your network is =
not
properly segmented
If you use fast ethernet, you might be able to send data at an average =
rate
of 70Mbit, or 9MByte. A single disk can transfer data at a sustained =
rate of
1.5 to 3 MByte per second. To be able to send enough data you either =
need
multiple users accessing different drives concurrently, or use disk =
striping
(RAID 0) or RAID 3 or 5. Don't blame the Alpha or WNT if your hardware
(disk) configuration cannot provide a bigger throughput.  (And don't use
striping without shadowing if you care about your data :-))

Lambert Hofstra
lamhof@thijssen.nl

>What you may want to consider is using some smaller, less capable =
system
set up to be your Domain Controller.  An i486 can easily handle 750 =
users
when it is only doing duty as a PDC or BDC.  Then set your 2100 up as a
SERVER not a DC.  I assume from your email address that these users will =
be
hitting this system for database apps [not MS SQL6.x!].  In this case =
get
your drive subsystem as fast as possible, either RAID 3 [faster reads] =
of
RAID 5 should do it.
