From jwl@ntmail.zso.dec.com Fri May 24 12:17:38 1996 CDT
Message-ID: <c=US%a=_%p=Digital%l=NTMAIL2-960524165732Z-5094@ntmail3.zso.dec.com>
Date: Fri, 24 May 1996 09:57:32 -0700
From: James Livingston <jwl@ntmail.zso.dec.com>
Subject: RE: Alpha 1000 video problems

Scott,

I'm sorry to report that, if there are any solutions to the performance
problems of the on-board video in the AlphaServer 1000, I'm not aware of
them.  The root of the problem is that the Cirrus Logic Linear 5422 is a
truly dumb frame buffer, and its driver has no hardware assists
available to it.  That's particularly troublesome when the application
is a 16-bit one, because the instructions that drive the frame buffer's
transfers for this application are excuted by the Intel 80286 emulator! 
You have seen the results.

The best solution for the problem is to get a cheap, ISA or PCI,
depending upon your slot usage, super VGA display adapter, e.g., a #9 S3
or such, and disable the on-board VGA.  That'll make all the difference,
at a cost of less than $100.00, max.  Jumper pins 2 & 3 of jumper J27 to
disable the on-board Cirrus chip.  Be sure, when you update the driver
for your replacement display adapter, that you don't disable the
existing adapter until you know your new one's working.

Cheers,
James

