From: dsorenson@mhasoftware.com <dsorenson@mhasoftware.com>
Subject: Multiple web sites on one NT box

rich <rich@promo.nucmed.buffalo.edu> asked:

>We are moving our WWW server from a Unix (Ultrix) server to our NT server.
> However, our current Unix server allows us to sponsor 2 other
>organizations.  Users can get to 1 of 3 locations using the following URL's:
>   www.nucmed.buffalo.edu        Our Department
>   www.nucmed.buffalo.edu:8000   Physicians for Social Responsibility
>   www.nucmed.buffalo.edu:8001   Buffalo Office of the Red Cross

	It appears that you have one host, and the web server is listening on
three separate ports.  This is not a problem for most web server software, but be
certain to check this out before ordering.  At my site we use Oracle webserver and
have found this to be a very simple thing to set up.

>Is there a way configure our NT Internet Server to allow us to use the above
>addresses.  Once we switch our campus domain name server to point to our NT
>Server and not our Unix server we will have lost the second 2 locations.

	Unless I mis-read, the DNS system should not have to be changed (other
than giving your new IP address for www.nucmed.buffalo.edu).  The host name portion
of the URL will remain the same, only the port number changes.

	One thing to note on setting up web servers: a number of products do what is
known as "virtual hosting," ie http://www.foo.com/ and http://www.bar.com/ will go to the
same webserver and be interpreted such that the server will send back two different pages.
This can be done on NT by placing two IP addresses on the TCP/IP protocol (done in
Network setup), and of course configuring DNS with your two addresses.  It can also be
done strictly in DNS, for that matter.  This is to be a standard part of the HTML specs for
the next release, I understand.  This may make for a more elegant solution than adding
the TCP/IP port to the URL, and might be worth looking into prior to setting up that server.

	Dan Sorenson
	Systems Admin, MHA Software Services, Inc.
	dsorenson@mhasoftware.com

